[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"System Design Tutorial: Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and Edge Computing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern distributed architectures require low latency, high availability, and scalable content delivery. Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and Edge Computing extend the traditional cloud infrastructure closer to the end-user, fundamentally changing how applications are designed and deployed."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Core Concepts of CDNs"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Content Delivery Network is a geographically distributed network of proxy servers and data centers (Points of Presence or PoPs). The primary goal is to cache static and dynamic content near the user to reduce latency, decrease load on origin servers, and mitigate DDoS attacks."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Origin Server: The authoritative server where the primary application code and database reside."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Edge Server (PoP): A proxy server located geographically closer to the end-user that caches content."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cache Hit Ratio (CHR): The percentage of requests served directly by the CDN edge without hitting the origin."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Evolution to Edge Computing"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"While traditional CDNs only cache and serve static assets (images, CSS, JS), Edge Computing allows developers to run compute logic (serverless functions) directly on the CDN edge servers. This enables real-time personalization, authentication, A/B testing, and API response manipulation right at the network perimeter."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. DNS Routing and Anycast"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"CDNs rely on advanced routing mechanisms to direct user traffic to the optimal edge location. Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) Anycast is widely used, where multiple routers advertise the exact same IP address. The internet routing infrastructure automatically routes the user packet to the topologically nearest PoP."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Cache Invalidation and Strategies"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Managing cache freshness is a critical system design challenge. Common strategies include Time-to-Live (TTL) expiration, Cache-Control headers, and explicit cache purging via API calls when data changes at the origin."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html
Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Edge Function Implementation Example"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of an edge worker script (similar to Cloudflare Workers or AWS CloudFront Functions) that intercepts a request, inspects a geolocation header, and personalizes the content before hitting the cache."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith(handleRequest(event.request));
});

async function handleRequest(request) {
  const country = request.headers.get('cf-ipcountry') || 'US';
  const response = await fetch(request);
  const clonedResponse = new Response(response.body, response);
  clonedResponse.headers.set('X-User-Country', country);
  return clonedResponse;
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"6. Trade-offs and Failure Modes"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stale Data: Aggressive caching can lead to users seeing outdated information until cache invalidation propagates."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cache Stampede (Thundering Herd): When a popular cache entry expires, concurrent requests flood the origin server simultaneously."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cold Starts at Edge: Running serverless compute at the edge may incur latency penalties on initial cold invocations."}]
